<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- MATH 14 - Review Problems #4, practice version: the same problems  -->
<!-- as review4-online.ptx, but with every <solution> removed and every  -->
<!-- <feedback> reduced to a neutral right/wrong message, so students     -->
<!-- can check their answers without seeing anything about the solutions. -->
<!-- This file is GENERATED-STYLE but maintained by hand: if the problems -->
<!-- in review4-online.ptx change, regenerate or mirror the change here. -->
<section xml:id="sec-exercises-review4-practice" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Review Problems #4</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Sections: <xref ref="section-stokes-theorem" text="title"/> and
      <xref ref="section-divergence-theorem" text="title"/>. Work each
      problem, then type your answers into the boxes and have them checked
      here.
    </p>
    <p>
      Press <q>Check Me</q> to submit; you may check as often as you like.
    </p>
    <p>
      Checking an answer tells you whether it is right or wrong and
      nothing more<mdash/>use the boxes to test yourself. A complete
      worked solution for every problem will be revealed beneath it
      once solutions are posted.
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>How to type your answers.</term>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Numbers.</term> Type a decimal number and nothing
          else<mdash/>no units, no <m>=</m> sign, no <m>\pi</m>. Give at
          least three decimal places (for instance <c>18.850</c>, not
          <c>6pi</c>). Answers are accepted within a small tolerance, so a
          little rounding is fine.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Expressions.</term> Type only the expression itself. Write
          products with <c>*</c> and powers with <c>^</c>, and write
          functions with parentheses: <c>cos(u)</c>, <c>sqrt(x^2+y^2)</c>,
          <c>e^z</c>. So the expression <m>2x + e^z</m> is typed
          <c>2*x + e^z</c>. Spaces are ignored.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Words.</term> Type just the word or letters asked for, in
          lower case unless the problem says otherwise.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </introduction>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-review4-practice">

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 1.  Verifying Stokes' theorem on a paraboloid  (RP4 #1)              -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-verify-paraboloid">
    <title>Verifying Stokes' Theorem on a Paraboloid</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="h" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="3" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="u" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="0" max="1" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="d" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="2" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-expression>a+(2*u-1)*d</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="kcomp" context="number">
        <de-expression>(2*u-1)*d</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="value" context="number">
        <de-expression>pi*h*(2*u-1)*d</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Confirm that Stokes' Theorem holds for
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \left\langle <eval obj="a"/>\left(y+z\right),\
        <eval obj="b"/>x,\ <eval obj="c"/>yz \right\rangle
      </me>
      <p>
        and the surface <m>S</m> that is the portion of the paraboloid
        <m>z = x^2+y^2</m> with <m>0 \le z \le <eval obj="h"/></m>,
        oriented with <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> pointing upward and its
        boundary <m>C</m> traversed counterclockwise as seen from above.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> The <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component of
        <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}</m> is
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="kcomp" width="10"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Give the common value of the two sides,
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \iint_S \left(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}\right) \cdot
        \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma = </m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="value" width="14"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="kcomp.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(kcomp.value({})))" max="kcomp.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(kcomp.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="value.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(value.value({})))" max="value.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(value.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        Writing <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m> for the three coefficients,
      </p>
      <me>
        \nabla \times \mathbf{F}
        = \left\langle cz,\ a,\ b-a \right\rangle ,
      </me>
      <p>
        so the <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component is
        <m>b-a = <eval obj="kcomp"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>The line integral.</term> The plane
        <m>z = <eval obj="h"/></m> meets the paraboloid in the circle
        <m>x^2+y^2 = <eval obj="h"/></m>, so <m>C</m> has radius
        <m>R = \sqrt{<eval obj="h"/>}</m>. With
        <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle R\cos t,\ R\sin t,\
        <eval obj="h"/> \right\rangle</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \int_0^{2\pi} \left(-aR^2\sin^2 t - a<eval obj="h"/>R\sin t
          + bR^2\cos^2 t\right) dt
        = \pi R^2 (b-a) .
      </me>
      <p>
        <term>The surface integral.</term> For the graph
        <m>z = g(x,y) = x^2+y^2</m> the upward normal is
        <m>\mathbf{n} = \mathbf{r}_x \times \mathbf{r}_y
        = \left\langle -2x,\ -2y,\ 1 \right\rangle</m>, so on <m>S</m>
      </p>
      <me>
        \left(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}\right) \cdot \mathbf{n}
        = -2cx\left(x^2+y^2\right) - 2ay + (b-a) .
      </me>
      <p>
        The first term is odd in <m>x</m> and the second is odd in
        <m>y</m>, so both integrate to zero over the disk
        <m>R</m> of radius <m>\sqrt{<eval obj="h"/>}</m>, leaving
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_S \left(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}\right) \cdot
        \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = (b-a)\iint_R dx\, dy = (b-a)\,\pi\,<eval obj="h"/> .
      </me>
      <p>
        The two sides agree, as <xref ref="eq-stokes"/> promises. Notice
        that <m><eval obj="c"/></m> never reaches the answer: it rides on
        the <m>\mathbf{i}</m>-component of the curl, which is killed by
        the symmetry of the disk.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 2.  Circulation of a planar field around two circles  (RP4 #2)       -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-two-circles-planar">
    <title>Circulation of a Planar Field Around Two Circles</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="r" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="3" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="kcomp" context="number">
        <de-expression>b+1</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="circA" context="number">
        <de-expression>-(b+1)*pi*r^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="zero" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \left\langle <eval obj="a"/>x - <eval obj="b"/>y,\
        x + <eval obj="c"/>y,\ 0 \right\rangle .
      </me>
      <p>
        Use Stokes' Theorem to make the following circulation
        calculations. Each circle <m>C</m> has radius
        <m><eval obj="r"/></m> and is centred at the origin.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> The <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component of
        <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}</m> is
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="kcomp" width="10"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> For <m>C</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane, oriented
        clockwise as viewed from the positive <m>z</m>-axis,
        <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r} = </m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="circA" width="14"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part C.</term> For <m>C</m> in the <m>yz</m>-plane, oriented
        clockwise as viewed from the positive <m>x</m>-axis,
        <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r} = </m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="number" ansobj="zero" width="14"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="kcomp.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(kcomp.value({})))" max="kcomp.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(kcomp.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="circA.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circA.value({})))" max="circA.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circA.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="zero.value({})-0.01" max="zero.value({})+0.01"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        The field has no <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component and neither component
        involves <m>z</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \nabla \times \mathbf{F}
        = \left\langle 0,\ 0,\ 1 - \left(-<eval obj="b"/>\right)
        \right\rangle
        = \left\langle 0,\ 0,\ <eval obj="kcomp"/> \right\rangle ,
      </me>
      <p>
        a constant field pointing along <m>\mathbf{k}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Take <m>S</m> to be the disk the circle
        bounds. Clockwise as seen from the positive <m>z</m>-axis is the
        orientation whose normal points <em>down</em>, so
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = -\mathbf{k}</m> and
        <m>\left(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}\right) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}
        = -<eval obj="kcomp"/></m>. By <xref ref="eq-stokes"/>,
      </p>
      <me>
        \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = -<eval obj="kcomp"/> \iint_S d\sigma
        = -<eval obj="kcomp"/>\,\pi\left(<eval obj="r"/>\right)^2 .
      </me>
      <p>
        <term>Part C.</term> Now <m>S</m> is a disk in the
        <m>yz</m>-plane, whose normal points along <m>\mathbf{i}</m>. The
        curl has no <m>\mathbf{i}</m>-component, so
        <m>\left(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}\right) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}
        = 0</m> at every point and the circulation is <m>0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The coefficients <m><eval obj="a"/></m> and
        <m><eval obj="c"/></m> never appear: they multiply <m>x</m> in
        the <m>\mathbf{i}</m>-component and <m>y</m> in the
        <m>\mathbf{j}</m>-component, and neither survives the curl.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 3.  A given curl around two circles  (RP4 #3)                        -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-curl-two-circles">
    <title>Circulation From a Given Curl</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="r" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="circA" context="number">
        <de-expression>c*pi*r^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="circB" context="number">
        <de-expression>pi*r^4/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose
      </p>
      <me>
        \nabla \times \mathbf{F}
        = \left\langle 0,\ x^2+z^2,\ <eval obj="c"/> \right\rangle .
      </me>
      <p>
        Find <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>,
        where <m>C</m> is a circle of radius <m><eval obj="r"/></m>
        centred at the origin, in each of the following positions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> <m>C</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane, oriented
        counterclockwise as viewed from above:
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="circA" width="14"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> <m>C</m> in the <m>xz</m>-plane, oriented
        counterclockwise as viewed from the positive <m>y</m>-axis:
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="circB" width="14"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="circA.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circA.value({})))" max="circA.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circA.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="circB.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circB.value({})))" max="circB.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(circB.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Let <m>S</m> be the disk the circle bounds in
        the <m>xy</m>-plane. Counterclockwise seen from above pairs with
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \mathbf{k}</m>, so only the
        <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component of the curl contributes and it is the
        constant <m><eval obj="c"/></m>. By <xref ref="eq-stokes"/>,
      </p>
      <me>
        \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \iint_S \left(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}\right) \cdot
          \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = <eval obj="c"/> \cdot \left(\text{area of } S\right)
        = <eval obj="c"/>\,\pi\left(<eval obj="r"/>\right)^2 .
      </me>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Now <m>S</m> is the disk in the
        <m>xz</m>-plane, with <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \mathbf{j}</m> and
        <m>d\sigma = dx\, dz</m>, so only the
        <m>\mathbf{j}</m>-component contributes. In polar coordinates on
        that disk, with <m>x^2+z^2 = \rho^2</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \iint_S \left(x^2+z^2\right) dx\, dz
        = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{<eval obj="r"/>} \rho^2\,\rho
          \, d\rho\, d\theta
        = \frac{\pi \left(<eval obj="r"/>\right)^4}{2} .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 4.  Divergence theorem on a box  (RP4 #4)                            -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-box-flux">
    <title>Outward Flux Through the Surface of a Box</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="A" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="p" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="q" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="s" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="zero" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="divF" context="formula">
        <de-expression>2*A*x+e^z</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>A*p^2*q*s+p*q*(e^s-1)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="sumF" context="formula">
        <de-expression>A*x^2+z^4+e^z</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate the <alert>outward</alert> flux of the vector field
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = <eval obj="A"/>x^2\,\mathbf{i}
        + z^4\,\mathbf{j} + e^z\,\mathbf{k}
      </me>
      <p>
        through the surface <m>S</m> of the solid box <m>W</m> given by
        <m>0 \le x \le <eval obj="p"/></m>,
        <m>0 \le y \le <eval obj="q"/></m>,
        <m>0 \le z \le <eval obj="s"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> By the Divergence Theorem the flux equals
        <m>\displaystyle\int_{a}^{b}\!\int_{c}^{d}\!\int_{e}^{f}</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="divF" width="14"/>
        <m>\,dx\,dy\,dz</m>, where
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>a =</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="zero" width="4"/>,
        <m>b =</m> <fillin name="b3" mode="number" ansobj="s" width="4"/>,
        <m>c =</m> <fillin name="b4" mode="number" ansobj="zero" width="4"/>,
        <m>d =</m> <fillin name="b5" mode="number" ansobj="q" width="4"/>,
        <m>e =</m> <fillin name="b6" mode="number" ansobj="zero" width="4"/>, and
        <m>f =</m> <fillin name="b7" mode="number" ansobj="p" width="4"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Evaluate the integral that you set up in the
        previous part.
      </p>
      <p>
        Flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b8" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <mathcmp obj="sumF"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="zero.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))" max="zero.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="s.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(s.value({})))" max="s.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(s.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="zero.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))" max="zero.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="q.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(q.value({})))" max="q.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(q.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="zero.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))" max="zero.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(zero.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="p.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(p.value({})))" max="p.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(p.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        By the Divergence Theorem,
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = \iiint_W \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\,dV</m> with
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 2Ax + e^z</m>, where <m>A</m> is the
        coefficient of <m>x^2</m>. Writing the triple integral in the order
        <m>dx\,dy\,dz</m>, the limits are the <m>z</m>-limits outside, then
        the <m>y</m>-limits, then the <m>x</m>-limits inside. Integrating,
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{Flux} = A p^2 q s + p q \left(e^{s} - 1\right),
      </me>
      <p>
        where <m>p, q, s</m> are the <m>x</m>-, <m>y</m>- and
        <m>z</m>-dimensions of the box.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>A = 3</m> and the box is <m>2 \times 3 \times 1</m>, so the flux is
<m>3\left(2^2\right)(3)(1) + (2)(3)\left(e - 1\right) = 30 + 6e \approx 46.31</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 5.  Maximizing box flux from a given divergence  (RP4 #5)            -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-box-div-max">
    <title>The Box With the Largest Flux</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="A" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="k" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="twok" context="number">
        <de-expression>2*k</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="M" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="10" max="15" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="fluxformula" context="formula">
        <de-expression>k*(2*A*a-a^2)*b*c</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="maxflux" context="number">
        <de-expression>k*A^2*M^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
        = <eval obj="twok"/>\left(<eval obj="A"/> - x\right)</m> and
        <m>0 \le a, b, c \le <eval obj="M"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (a) Find the flux of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> out of the rectangular solid
        <m>0 \le x \le a</m>, <m>0 \le y \le b</m>, <m>0 \le z \le c</m>,
        as an expression in <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>c</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="fluxformula" width="20"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) For what values of <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m> is the flux
        largest?
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>a =</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="A" width="6"/>,
        <m>b =</m> <fillin name="b3" mode="number" ansobj="M" width="6"/>,
        <m>c =</m> <fillin name="b4" mode="number" ansobj="M" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) What is that largest flux?
      </p>
      <p>
        flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b5" mode="number" ansobj="maxflux" width="10"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="A.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(A.value({})))" max="A.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(A.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp object="M" min="M.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(M.value({})))" max="M.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(M.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="M.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(M.value({})))" max="M.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(M.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="M.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(M.value({})))" max="M.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(M.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="maxflux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(maxflux.value({})))" max="maxflux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(maxflux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        By the Divergence Theorem the flux out of the box is
        <m>\displaystyle\int_0^{c}\!\!\int_0^{b}\!\!\int_0^{a}
        2k\left(A - x\right)dx\,dy\,dz = k\left(2Aa - a^2\right)bc</m>,
        where <m>2k</m> is the leading coefficient and <m>A</m> the root of
        the divergence. Since <m>b</m> and <m>c</m> multiply a positive
        quantity, take <m>b = c = M</m>, the largest allowed value; and
        <m>2Aa - a^2</m> is maximized at <m>a = A</m>. The largest flux is
        <m>k A^2 M^2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>2k = 4</m>, <m>A = 4</m> and <m>M = 12</m>, so the flux is
<m>2\left(8a - a^2\right)bc</m>, largest at <m>a = 4</m>, <m>b = c = 12</m>,
where it equals <m>2\left(4^2\right)\left(12^2\right) = 4608</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 6.  Verify the Divergence Theorem on a cylinder  (RP4 #6)            -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-verify-cylinder">
    <title>Verifying the Divergence Theorem on a Cylinder</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="A" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="B" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="C" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="h" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="both" context="number">
        <de-expression>A*h*pi</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify the Divergence Theorem for the vector field and region:
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \langle <eval obj="A"/>x,\ <eval obj="B"/>z,\
        <eval obj="C"/>y \rangle
      </me>
      <p>
        and the region <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 1</m>,
        <m>0 \le z \le <eval obj="h"/></m>. Give each side as a decimal
        number.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{S} \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="both" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iiint_{W} \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\ dV =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="both" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="both.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(both.value({})))" max="both.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(both.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="both.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(both.value({})))" max="both.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(both.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        With <m>\mathbf{F} = \langle Ax, Bz, Cy\rangle</m> we get
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = A</m>, so
        <m>\iiint_W \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\,dV
        = A\,\text{Vol}(W) = A\pi h</m>. Computing the surface integral
        directly: on the side, <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}
        = A\cos^2\theta + Bz\sin\theta</m> integrates to <m>A h\pi</m>; the
        top and bottom disks contribute <m>0</m> by symmetry, since there
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \pm Cy</m>. Both sides equal
        <m>A h \pi</m>, verifying the theorem.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>A = 3</m> and <m>h = 2</m>, so both sides equal
<m>3(2)\pi = 6\pi \approx 18.85</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 7.  Divergence theorem on a sphere  (RP4 #7)                         -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-sphere">
    <title>Flux Through a Sphere</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="R" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="Rsq" context="number">
        <de-expression>R^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="divF" context="formula">
        <de-expression>3*x^2+3*z^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>8*pi*R^5/5</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the Divergence Theorem to evaluate the surface integral
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{S} \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma</m>, where
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \langle x^3,\ <eval obj="c"/>,\ z^3 \rangle
      </me>
      <p>
        and <m>S</m> is the sphere
        <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = <eval obj="Rsq"/></m>, oriented outward.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="divF" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{S} \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 3x^2 + 3z^2</m>. By symmetry of the
        ball <m>W</m> of radius <m>R</m>, the integrals of <m>x^2</m>,
        <m>y^2</m>, <m>z^2</m> over <m>W</m> are all equal to
        <m>\frac13\iiint_W \rho^2\,dV
        = \frac13\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi}\!\!\int_0^{R}
        \rho^4\sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta = \frac{4\pi R^5}{15}</m>.
        Hence
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = \iiint_W 3\left(x^2 + z^2\right)dV
        = 6\cdot\frac{4\pi R^5}{15} = \frac{8\pi R^5}{5}.
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>R = 3</m>, so the flux is
<m>\dfrac{8\pi\left(3^5\right)}{5} = \dfrac{1944\pi}{5} \approx 1221.45</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 8.  Radial field through a cylinder on the x-axis  (RP4 #8)          -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-cylinder-x-axis">
    <title>Radial Field Through a Closed Cylinder</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="R" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="L" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="three" context="number">
        <de-expression>3</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>6*pi*R^2*L</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the flux of the vector field
        <m>\mathbf{F} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
        through the surface <m>S</m>, which is a closed cylinder of radius
        <m><eval obj="R"/></m> centered on the <m>x</m>-axis, with
        <m>-<eval obj="L"/> \le x \le <eval obj="L"/></m>, and oriented
        outward.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="three" width="6"/>
        (a number)
      </p>
      <p>
        Flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="three.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(three.value({})))" max="three.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(three.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        The surface is closed, so by the Divergence Theorem with
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 3</m>,
        <m>\text{Flux} = 3\,\text{Vol}(W) = 3\,\pi R^2 (2L) = 6\pi R^2 L</m>,
        where <m>R</m> is the radius and <m>2L</m> the length of the
        cylinder.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>R = 3</m> and <m>L = 2</m>, so the flux is
<m>6\pi\left(3^2\right)(2) = 108\pi \approx 339.29</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 9.  A family of radial fields r^A (x i + y j)  (RP4 #9)              -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-radial-power">
    <title>Divergence of a Family of Radial Fields</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="pp" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="qq" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="a1" context="number">
        <de-expression>-2+pp</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="a2" context="number">
        <de-expression>-2-qq</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="coeff" context="formula">
        <de-expression>2+A</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="expo" context="formula">
        <de-expression>A</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="minustwo" context="number">
        <de-expression>-2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>r = \left(x^2+y^2\right)^{1/2}</m> and consider the vector
        field <m>\mathbf{F} = r^A\!\left(x\,\mathbf{i} +
        y\,\mathbf{j}\right)</m>, where <m>r \ne 0</m> and <m>A</m> is a
        constant. <m>\mathbf{F}</m> has no <m>z</m>-component and is
        independent of <m>z</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (a) Find <m>\nabla \cdot \left(r^A\!\left(x\,\mathbf{i}
        + y\,\mathbf{j}\right)\right)</m>, and show that it can be written
        in the form <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = c\,r^{a}</m>. As
        expressions in <m>A</m>:
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>c =</m> <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="coeff" width="8"/>, and
        <m>a =</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="expo" width="8"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) For what values of <m>A</m> is <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}</m>
        positive, negative, or zero?
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}</m> is positive for <m>A \gt</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="number" ansobj="minustwo" width="6"/>,
        negative for <m>A \lt</m>
        <fillin name="b4" mode="number" ansobj="minustwo" width="6"/>, and
        zero for <m>A =</m>
        <fillin name="b5" mode="number" ansobj="minustwo" width="6"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) Suppose that <m>A = <eval obj="a1"/></m>. What is the sign of
        the flux out of a small sphere centered at <m>(1,1,1)</m>? Type
        <c>positive</c> or <c>negative</c>:
        <fillin name="b6" mode="string" answer="positive" width="10"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        If instead <m>A = <eval obj="a2"/></m>, what is the sign of the
        flux out of a small sphere centered at <m>(1,1,1)</m>?
        <fillin name="b7" mode="string" answer="negative" width="10"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>(Be sure you can say how your answers in part (c) would change
        if the question were about a small sphere centered at
        <m>(0,0,0)</m>.)</em>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="minustwo.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(minustwo.value({})))" max="minustwo.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(minustwo.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="minustwo.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(minustwo.value({})))" max="minustwo.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(minustwo.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="minustwo.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(minustwo.value({})))" max="minustwo.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(minustwo.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <strcmp case="insensitive">positive</strcmp>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <strcmp case="insensitive">negative</strcmp>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        (a) With <m>r_x = x/r</m> and <m>r_y = y/r</m>, the product rule
        gives <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 2r^A + A r^{A-1}
        \left(\tfrac{x^2+y^2}{r}\right) = (2+A)\,r^A</m>, so
        <m>c = 2+A</m> and <m>a = A</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) Since <m>r^A \gt 0</m>, the divergence is positive for
        <m>A \gt -2</m>, negative for <m>A \lt -2</m>, and zero for
        <m>A = -2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) The flux out of a small sphere has the sign of the divergence
        at its center, provided the sphere avoids the origin, where the
        field is undefined: positive when <m>A \gt -2</m>, negative when
        <m>A \lt -2</m>. For a sphere centered at the origin the
        calculation above says nothing, since the divergence is not
        defined there.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>A = 2 \gt -2</m> the flux out of a small sphere at <m>(1,1,1)</m> is
positive; for <m>A = -5 \lt -2</m> it is negative.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 10.  Flux out of a closed cone  (RP4 #10)                            -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-cone">
    <title>Flux Out of a Closed Cone</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="B" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="C" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="r" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="divF" context="formula">
        <de-expression>2*a*y</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>a*r^4*pi/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the flux of
        <m>\mathbf{F} = <eval obj="B"/>z\,\mathbf{i}
        + <eval obj="a"/>y^2\,\mathbf{j} + <eval obj="C"/>x\,\mathbf{k}</m>
        out of the closed cone <m>y = \sqrt{x^2 + z^2}</m>, with
        <m>0 \le y \le <eval obj="r"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="divF" width="10"/>
        (an expression in <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, <m>z</m>)
      </p>
      <p>
        flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 2ay</m>, where <m>a</m> is the
        coefficient of <m>y^2</m>. By the Divergence Theorem, using
        cylindrical coordinates around the <m>y</m>-axis (the cone is
        <m>0 \le \rho \le y \le r</m>),
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{flux} = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{r}\!\!\int_0^{y}
        2ay\,\rho\,d\rho\,dy\,d\theta
        = 2\pi\int_0^{r} a y^3\,dy = \frac{a r^4 \pi}{2}.
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 3</m> and <m>r = 2</m>, so the flux is
<m>\dfrac{3\left(2^4\right)\pi}{2} = 24\pi \approx 75.40</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 11.  Divergence theorem on a tetrahedron  (RP4 #11)                  -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-tetrahedron">
    <title>Flux Through the Surface of a Tetrahedron</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="divF" context="formula">
        <de-expression>1+x</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>a*b*c*(a+4)/24</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="vol" context="number">
        <de-expression>a*b*c/6</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the Divergence Theorem to calculate the flux of
        <m>\mathbf{F}</m> across <m>S</m>, where
        <m>\mathbf{F} = z\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} +
        zx\,\mathbf{k}</m> and <m>S</m> is the surface of the tetrahedron
        enclosed by the coordinate planes and the plane
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{x}{<eval obj="a"/>} + \frac{y}{<eval obj="b"/>}
        + \frac{z}{<eval obj="c"/>} = 1.
      </me>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="divF" width="10"/>
        (an expression in <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, <m>z</m>)
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp object="vol" min="vol.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(vol.value({})))" max="vol.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(vol.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 0 + 1 + x = 1 + x</m>. The
        tetrahedron <m>E</m> has vertices <m>(0,0,0)</m>,
        <m>(a,0,0)</m>, <m>(0,b,0)</m>, <m>(0,0,c)</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = \iiint_E (1+x)\,dV
        = \frac{bc}{2}\int_0^{a}(1+x)\left(1-\frac{x}{a}\right)^2 dx
        = \frac{abc\,(a+4)}{24},
      </me>
      <p>
        using <m>\int_0^{a}(1+x)\left(1-\tfrac{x}{a}\right)^2 dx
        = \tfrac{a}{3} + \tfrac{a^2}{12} = \tfrac{a(a+4)}{12}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 3</m>, <m>b = 2</m> and <m>c = 4</m>, so the flux is
<m>\dfrac{(3)(2)(4)(3+4)}{24} = 7</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  </exercises>
</section>
